    "text": "Liquids <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> form an ideal solution in the entire composition range. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>350</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>,</mo></math> the vapour pressure of pure A and pure B are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>Pa</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>Pa</mi></math> , respectively. The composition of the vapour in equilibrium with a solution containing 40 mole percent of A at this temperature is:",
